  "content": "# Why Database Indexes Speed Up Reads but Slow Down Writes\n\n## The Core Idea\n\nAn index is a **separate data structure** (usually a B-tree) that maps column values to the physical locations of rows. It's like the index at the back of a textbook: instead of scanning every page to find a topic, you look it up alphabetically and jump straight to the right page.\n\nThat extra structure is what creates the trade-off:\n\n- **Reads get faster** because you can find rows without scanning the whole table.\n- **Writes get slower** because every insert, update, or delete must also update the index (or indexes) to keep them consistent with the table.\n\n---\n\n## Why Reads Speed Up\n\nWithout an index, a query like:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'alice@example.com';\n```\n\nforces a **full table scan** — the database reads every row until it finds a match. With 10 million rows, that's 10 million row reads.\n\nWith an index on `email`, the database walks a B-tree (typically ~3–4 levels deep for millions of rows) and lands directly on the matching row. That's a handful of page reads instead of millions.\n\n---\n\n## Why",
